<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tolistobogii</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tolistoboii</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tolistobogii were one of the three Celtic tribes of Galatia in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, alongside the Tectosages and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trocmi">Trocmi</a>. They crossed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> in 278 BCE and settled the western part of Galatia around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gordion">Gordion</a> and Pessinus. Like the other tribes they were divided into four tetrarchies, and their tetrarch <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deiotarus">Deiotarus</a> became king of all Galatia in the 1st century BCE.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name appears in two forms: Tolistobogioi, used by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a>, and Tolistoagioi, found chiefly in inscriptions; both occur in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a> and are generally taken to denote one people. The second element is usually read as Celtic, perhaps bogio-, breakers, or agio-, fighters, while the first, tolisto-, has no accepted etymology, and Alexander Falileyev observes that it may not be Celtic at all.</p><p>The Tolistobogii reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> in the migration of Celtic peoples from the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> in the early 3rd century BCE. After the attacks on Macedonia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, the Galatians crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellespont">Hellespont</a> through an alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicomedes-i-of-bithynia">Nicomedes I of Bithynia</a> and were settled in north-central Anatolia by the end of the 260s BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 189 BCE, during the campaign of the Roman commander Gnaeus Manlius Vulso, the Tolistobogii under Ortiagon, joined by the fighting men of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trocmi">Trocmi</a>, gathered their whole people on Mount Olympus and fortified it with ditches and ramparts. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> stormed the position, inflicting heavy losses and taking as many as 40,000 captive. Ortiagon afterwards made himself sole ruler of the region for a time but was defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eumenes-ii">Eumenes II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon">Pergamon</a>.</p><p>In the later 2nd century BCE the chieftain Sinorix was a dominant figure, and his authority passed to his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deiotarus">Deiotarus</a>, whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> rewarded with territory and the title of king. After the last Galatian king, Amyntas, was killed in about 25 BCE, Galatia became a Roman province, with Pessinus one of its three centres beside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankara">Ancyra</a> and Tavium.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The westernmost of the three Galatian peoples, the Tolistobogii held northern and western Galatia, reaching west across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sakarya-river">Sangarius</a> on both sides of the Tembris, the modern Porsuk, as far as Phrygia Epictetus. The temple-state of Pessinus, sanctuary of the mother goddess, was their chief market. Three of their tetrarchies are known by name, the Ambitouti, the Tosiopes and the Voturi, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deiotarus">Deiotarus</a> kept his residence at Blucium and his treasury at Peium.</p><p>Their territory holds the most impressive Galatian remains: stone chambered tombs under earthen tumuli, of a type also found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bithynia">Bithynia</a> and Pontus, with the finest at Karalar, where an inscription names the burial of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deiotarus">Deiotarus</a> the Younger, son and co-regent of Deiotarus, who died between 43 and 41 BCE.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tolistobogii” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Three tribes, the <b>Tolistobogii</b>, Tectosages and Trocmi, each split into four parts under tetrarchs, met in a council of 300 at a sacred grove called Drynemeton.</blockquote><a href="/state/galatia">Galatia</a></li></ul></section>
